rationale:
**Obligation.** When a response has exactly one `submit_result` call, any arguments other than an object containing only `result` must end the request with `ValidationError::Agent`. Decoding the `result` value into `R` is delegated to the cited `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.submission.decoding`.

**Envelope check.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tools.rs#mod[submission_tests]/fn[submission_envelopes_require_one_result_field]` tests `submitted_value`, the function that makes this check. `submitted_value` accepts only an object with exactly one key, `result`. The test feeds it these invalid inputs and asserts the exact error message `submit_result requires exactly result` for each:
- non-objects: null, array, string and boolean;
- an empty object;
- an object with a wrong key;
- an object with `result` plus an extra key.

It also asserts that valid envelopes return the inner value unchanged, including falsy and structured values such as null, `[]`, a nested object and `false`.

**End-to-end check.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[typed_submission_references_resolve_and_invalid_results_are_agent_errors]` runs the full agent loop through `ToolAgent` with a scripted provider. It sends single `submit_result` calls with these arguments and asserts `Err(ValidationError::Agent(_))` for each:
- `{}`;
- `{"other":true}`;
- `{"result":true,"extra":true}`.

These cover the missing-field, wrong-field and extra-field envelope cases at the request level, which confirms the error maps to `ValidationError::Agent`. The fourth case, `{"result":"wrong"}` asked as `bool`, is a decoding failure. It is covered here as context for the cited decoding requirement and is not part of this subject's own obligation.

**Remaining gap check.** The non-object envelope cases (null, array, string and boolean) are only tested at the unit level. I found no conditional between `submitted_value` and the `ValidationError::Agent` conversion that would treat them differently from the end-to-end cases. The only code I read along that path was `GraphSession::submit` in `tools.rs`, which calls `submitted_value`. I did not read the conversion to `ValidationError::Agent` itself. The end-to-end test shows it for the envelope cases it runs.

Both tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. I found no adequacy gap, and no acknowledgments apply.
